    "content": {
      "question_html": "<p>Light of wavelength 400 nm is incident on a metal surface with a work function of 2.3 eV.</p>\n<p>h = 6.63 × 10<sup>−34</sup> J s, c = 3.00 × 10<sup>8</sup> m s<sup>−1</sup>, e = 1.60 × 10<sup>−19</sup> C.</p>\n<div class=\"q-part\"><span class=\"q-part__label\">(a)</span><div class=\"q-part__body\">Calculate the energy of a photon of this light in eV.</div><span class=\"q-marks\">(2)</span></div>\n<div class=\"q-part\"><span class=\"q-part__label\">(b)</span><div class=\"q-part__body\">Calculate the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons in eV.</div><span class=\"q-marks\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"q-part\"><span class=\"q-part__label\">(c)</span><div class=\"q-part__body\">Explain why increasing the intensity of the light does not change this maximum kinetic energy.</div><span class=\"q-marks\">(2)</span></div>",
      "question_image_url": null,
      "mark_scheme_html": "<div class=\"q-part\"><span class=\"q-part__label\">(a)</span><div class=\"q-part__body\">E = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"frac__n\">hc</span><span class=\"frac__d\">λ</span></span> = 4.97 × 10<sup>−19</sup> J</div><span class=\"ms-codes\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"q-line\"><div class=\"q-line__text\">= 3.1 eV</div><span class=\"ms-codes\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"q-part\"><span class=\"q-part__label\">(b)</span><div class=\"q-part__body\">3.1 − 2.3 = 0.8 eV</div><span class=\"ms-codes\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"q-part\"><span class=\"q-part__label\">(c)</span><div class=\"q-part__body\">Each electron absorbs one photon, and the photon energy depends only on frequency</div><span class=\"ms-codes\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"q-line\"><div class=\"q-line__text\">Greater intensity means more photons per second, so more electrons, not more energy per electron</div><span class=\"ms-codes\">(1)</span></div>",
      "mark_scheme_image_url": null
    }
  },
